  "final_answer": "I found the answer on the ohmyzsh Themes wiki page. Here's how to change the zsh theme to agnoster:\n\n## How to Change the ZSH Theme to Agnoster\n\nAccording to the official ohmyzsh wiki page on Themes (https://github.com/ohmyzsh/ohmyzsh/wiki/Themes), the instructions are:\n\n1. **Open your `~/.zshrc` file** in your preferred editor (e.g., `nano ~/.zshrc` or `vim ~/.zshrc`).\n\n2. **Set the `ZSH_THEME` variable** to `agnoster`. Find the line that currently sets `ZSH_THEME` (it will likely be `ZSH_THEME=\"robbyrussell\"` by default) and change it to:\n   ```\n   ZSH_THEME=\"agnoster\"\n   ```\n   This should be set **before** the line that sources Oh My Zsh.\n\n3. **Save the file** and **restart your terminal**, or apply the change immediately by running:\n   ```\n   source ~/.zshrc\n   ```\n\n### Important Note from the Wiki\nThe wiki specifically warns that **the agnoster theme requires a Powerline Font or a Nerd Font** to be installed in order to render properly. Without one of these fonts installed, you will see weird/weird-looking prompt symbols (boxes, question marks, or missing glyphs) instead of the intended styling. You can find installation instructions for these fonts in the [FAQ](https://github.com/ohmyzsh/ohmyzsh/wiki/FAQ) on the ohmyzsh wiki.\n\n### Additional Info\n- The `agnoster` theme is one of the bundled themes that ships with Oh My Zsh (located in the `themes/` directory of the Oh My Zsh distribution).\n- If you want to disable any theme, set `ZSH_THEME=\"\"`.\n- If you set `ZSH_THEME=\"random\"`, a random theme will be loaded each time you start a shell session.",
